Correction: the stale-binary claim in this message is wrong.

`~/.cargo/bin/b10x-harness` (2026-08-24) is what `command -v` finds on the
operator's PATH, and it is what this script's old precondition checked. It is
NOT what ran. `metaharness` resolves the child's program against a PATH it
constructs itself — `child_path()` in metaharness-b10x is
`$HOME/.local/bin:BASE_PATH`, deliberately not the operator's, so that `doctor`
and the spawn agree on which install answered (builder.rs:534, CT-3).
`~/.local/bin/b10x-harness` was built 2026-08-29 09:47 and there are no harness
commits after it, so the arm ran a current binary and every b10x result stands
on that count.

What is true, and was the whole cause: substrate published no `run` because the
calling process's cgroup was outside the configured root.

Two consequences for the change this commit made:

  * the PATH prepend it adds does nothing for the spawn — metaharness ignores
    the operator's PATH by design — so it only ever affected `command -v` and
    the catalogue probe;
  * worse, it made the probe test a *different* binary from the one that runs,
    which is the opposite of what a probe is for.

Both are fixed in the commit that follows this note.

The verified facts, so a reader does not have to re-derive them:
  ~/.cargo/bin/b10x-harness   2026-08-24 15:33  on operator PATH, never spawned
  ~/.local/bin/b10x-harness   2026-08-29 09:47  resolved by child_path, spawned
  harness commits after 09:47: none
